<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 2</title></head><body><h1>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 2</h1><p>Paul Banyard — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 2)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>03-11 Weekly Review</p><p>Plaud file id: 70ed7b1e4fc7b627fbea82bb3564de15</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Weekly Performance and Pipeline Review</p><p>This document outlines a performance review meeting focused on Paul Banyard&#x27;s weekly workload, overdue tasks, and financial metrics. The discussion, led by Bob Duncan, analyses Paul&#x27;s strong mortgage pipeline against a significant gap in insurance sales, leading to a strategic directive to prioritise protection business and refine collaboration with his support, Sean McLennan, to manage the high volume of work effectively.</p><p>## Weekly Mortgage Pipeline and Workload</p><p>Paul Banyard commenced the review by detailing a substantial influx of mortgage business, describing the current week&#x27;s outlook as &quot;mortgage wise good.&quot; He outlined a robust pipeline that includes a remortgage application with a £5,400 prop fee, a buy-to-let remortgage, and a small £100k remortgage for a client named Raymond. Additionally, he highlighted high-value potential business, including an appointment with a client named Mendon who has had an offer accepted on a £600k property, and another residential purchase referral from Gavin at Edinburgh involving a property valued at approximately £1 million.</p><p>The workload is significant, with Paul identifying potentially seven applications to process this week—one already submitted and six pending. This volume requires extensive administrative effort, including seven sets of notes and reports. Paul estimated this administrative burden would constitute a &quot;solid day&#x27;s work,&quot; calculating approximately six to seven hours based on an allocation of one hour plus per client. He also noted a completed protection review for a client whose mortgage was finalised the previous Friday, though the sheer volume of mortgage applications poses a time management challenge.</p><p>## Workflow Management and Overdue Task Review</p><p>Bob Duncan conducted a scrutiny of Paul&#x27;s workflow management within Midas Pro, specifically targeting a backlog of overdue tasks. Paul reported twelve items in his overdue tab, with the longest outstanding task dating back to the 5th of March—six days prior to the meeting. Bob characterised a six-day delay as &quot;too excessive,&quot; particularly for clients who were promised follow-ups, and urged Paul to clear these lists daily or keep overdues to a maximum of one or two days.</p><p>Specific overdue cases were examined to identify bottlenecks:</p><p>- **Document Requests:** Bob questioned whether initial documents had been requested for certain clients and if statuses were accurate.</p><p>- **Client Communication:** Paul noted &quot;radio silence&quot; from one client after sending a recap email. Bob suggested that if Paul struggles to make these chase calls due to workload, Sean should be utilised to reach out on his behalf.</p><p>- **Paul Clarkson:** This case involves a protection review without a mortgage attached; the client frequently becomes busy and &quot;drops off the face of the earth,&quot; stalling the collection of basic ID proofs.</p><p>- **Steph Mitchell:** Confirmed as Paul&#x27;s client (despite Bob initially thinking she was his), this case involves a product transfer with Newcastle.</p><p>- **Brianna Kennedy:** An EWS1 issue has been resolved, and a NatWest application was submitted, which will clear the overdue status.</p><p>Bob recommended a decisive daily approach: determine immediately if a task is a &quot;new job&quot; for Paul or a &quot;Sean job&quot; to ensure efficiently managed workflows.</p><p>## Analysis of Financial Performance and Key Metrics</p><p>The financial review highlighted a strong performance trajectory for Paul, particularly in mortgage writing. February concluded with a &quot;nice strong finish,&quot; and March has started positively with £6k in written business already recorded. Bob projected that Paul could write another £4k to £5k this week, potentially matching previous £6k weeks. Year-to-date, Paul has written £114k, surpassing last year&#x27;s total revenue of £100k with two months still remaining in the financial year. Bob noted that if momentum continues, Paul could add another £30k+, significantly outperforming his previous benchmarks.</p><p>Banked income remains robust. February saw a total just shy of £13k, with March currently showing over £6k projected and April sitting at £22k. This creates a quarterly banked income projection of roughly £40k to £48k, assuming successful banking of current applications and no major clawbacks.</p><p>However, a critical disparity was identified between mortgage volume and protection (insurance) sales. Paul currently holds 13 active mortgage applications (soon to be 20) against only four insurance cases. Bob warned that while one or two insurance cases can boost conversion stats to 40-46%, a high volume of mortgages without corresponding insurance drags the penetration rate down significantly. With a target of six insurance cases and an average premium of £126, there is a potential £16k in income at stake. Bob emphasised that protection income often equals or exceeds mortgage income—February saw £10k in protection versus £5k in mortgages—making it the primary financial driver to prioritise.</p><p>## Strategic Focus on Insurance and Renewal Opportunities</p><p>Bob Duncan issued a clear strategic directive: the immediate focus must shift to &quot;insurance first.&quot; He expressed concern that the high volume of mortgage administrative work might cause protection opportunities to slip, which would require difficult catch-up work later. The goal is to capitalise on the current mortgage influx to secure the identified £16k in potential protection revenue. If Paul successfully converts the outstanding insurance cases alongside his mortgage pipeline, Bob estimated a potential monthly revenue of £26k to £28k.</p><p>The discussion also covered renewal opportunities, which are viewed as a consistent revenue stream. Paul reported he is currently working on three to four renewal cases, including a product transfer for Stephanie Mitchell. Bob advised Sean to review the remortgage spreadsheet, specifically looking at the &quot;September bunch&quot; (loans ending in six months), to ensure these clients are being actively chased. This proactive management of renewals is expected to result in a busy December, counteracting the seasonal drop-off in purchase cases usually seen at the end of the year.</p><p>## Process Refinement and Team Collaboration</p><p>To manage the heavy workload and correct the insurance imbalance, the team discussed refining their collaborative process. Bob encouraged Paul to delegate more aggressively to Sean, stating, &quot;Don&#x27;t be afraid to ask the question.&quot; Specifically, routine tasks such as keying applications (a 5-10 minute job) or making initial chase calls should be passed to Sean to free up Paul&#x27;s time for high-value advisory work.</p><p>A specific gap was identified in the customer journey regarding insurance: while the system for booking the *first* appointment is solid, clients often drift &quot;into the dark&quot; afterwards. Sean expressed uncertainty about his role in chasing these clients post-appointment. To resolve this, Paul agreed to copy Sean into informal email communications and ensuring relevant updates are added to case notes, giving Sean the visibility needed to assist with follow-ups.</p><p>Additionally, urgent collaborative actions were agreed upon for two specific files: Jamie Braithwaite and Wendy Watson. Paul needs to push these applications to the provider site immediately so Sean can complete the health questions, ensuring the clients go on risk as soon as possible.</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Paul Banyard**</p><p>- [ ] Clear the 12 overdue items in Midas Pro - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Determine daily which overdue tasks can be delegated to Sean - [Daily]</p><p>- [ ] Update lead details for Angie Ray to reflect Megan as the owner of the B&amp;C review - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Copy Sean into email communications regarding protection bookings/follow-ups to ensure visibility - [Ongoing]</p><p>- [ ] Push applications for Jamie Braithwaite and Wendy Watson to the provider site - [ASAP]</p><p>- [ ] Establish a &quot;powwow&quot; with Sean to define the post-first-appointment insurance journey - [TBD]</p><p>**@Sean McLennan**</p><p>- [ ] Reach out to clients on the overdue list (specifically property hunters) on Paul’s behalf to check status - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Complete health questions for Jamie Braithwaite and Wendy Watson once applications are keyed - [ASAP]</p><p>- [ ] Review the remortgage spreadsheet (specifically the September cohort) to track renewal progress - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Chase up renewal clients from the spreadsheet who are currently listed as &quot;on a chaser&quot; - [TBD]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p>## Internal Performance Review — Paul Banyard (Review date: 2026-03-11)</p><p>### 1. Follow-up from Previous Meeting (actions were set)</p><p>#### Actions previously agreed</p><p>- Correct appointment/lead statuses: **Siobhan Duncan, Brianna (Kennedy), Jasmine, David Lee** and other misaligned records.</p><p>- Increase **DIP/Decision in Principle (“depth”)** progression for clients already in workflows.</p><p>- Update **statuses and notes immediately after appointments**.</p><p>- Update leads, escalate cases to **Sean**, and keep **Midas Pro workflows** current.</p><p>#### Completed vs outstanding</p><p>**Completed / progress made**</p><p>- Overdue workflow items reduced from **~20** (noted last week) to **12 overdue tasks** currently showing.</p><p>- **Brianna Kennedy**: **EWS1** issue resolved; **NatWest application submitted** (CRM/report update expected to reflect on the next report run).</p><p>- **Raymond**: documents received; moved back into today for progression.</p><p>**Outstanding**</p><p>- **12 overdue tasks** remain; oldest due date **2026-03-05** (**6 days overdue** as of **2026-03-11**).</p><p>- Several cases appear to be sitting in incorrect workflow stages (e.g., “create fact find” / “documents received” without logged doc requests, next steps, or re-diarised chases).</p><p>#### Reasons for delay (as stated)</p><p>- Workload pressure: up to **6–7 applications** to submit plus **6–7 notes/reports** (including one application already submitted but not fully noted/reported).</p><p>- Some clients are “slow burn” (not actively looking weekly), resulting in longer gaps between check-ins.</p><p>- **Radio silence** after recap emails with no structured re-chase logged.</p><p>#### Repeated missed actions / behaviour patterns</p><p>- **CRM/workflow drift**: items reaching **6+ days overdue** indicates repeated slippage in daily task clearing/decisioning.</p><p>- **Avoidance by deferral**: cases remain “in place” rather than being explicitly **escalated to Sean** for chase calls when adviser capacity is constrained.</p><p>- **Protection activity not proactively referenced** in weekly output (flagged by Bob as a consistent theme).</p><p>#### Ongoing blockers</p><p>- Capacity constraint (applications + notes/reports) is driving overdue accumulation and incomplete workflow hygiene.</p><p>#### Risk created by inaction</p><p>- **SLA/service risk**: follow-ups due **2026-03-05** not completed by **2026-03-11**.</p><p>- **Pipeline leakage**: clients may disengage if follow-ups are not timely (especially “property hunting” clients).</p><p>- **Reporting distortion**: incorrect staging/ownership (including B&amp;C/protection items) contaminates adviser stats and visibility.</p><p>**Action Owner:** Adviser (Paul) / Case Manager (Sean where escalated)</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **Improving, but below standard** — overdues reduced materially, but oldest items remain **6 days overdue**, indicating incomplete daily discipline.</p><p>---</p><p>### 2. Leads Performance Review (with CRM discipline)</p><p>#### C. Leads — Last Week</p><p>**Current Position**</p><p>- **10 new appointments/leads** last week.</p><p>- “Vast majority” updated; **1 lead** likely needs a status correction.</p><p>**Vs Last Week / Last Month**</p><p>- Not stated.</p><p>**What’s Working Well**</p><p>- Appointment volume (**10**) is strong vs recent weeks.</p><p>- Most records were progressed/updated rather than left untouched.</p><p>**What’s Not Working Well**</p><p>- At least **one** lead appears incorrectly staged/created vs actual outcome (admin noise / reporting risk).</p><p>**Risks / Blockers**</p><p>- Misownership (e.g., B&amp;C meant for **Megan**) creates **ownership confusion** and **misreporting**.</p><p>**Actions Required**</p><p>- Identify and correct any lead(s) from last week with the wrong **stage/owner**.</p><p>**Action Owner:** Adviser (Paul)</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **Improving** — activity volume and basic updates are better; accuracy/ownership discipline still inconsistent.</p><p>#### D. Leads — This Week (to 2026-03-11)</p><p>**Current Position**</p><p>- **4 appointments held** so far:</p><p>  - **3 on 2026-03-09**</p><p>  - **1 on 2026-03-10**</p><p>- **One appointment** was effectively **Karen’s client** (first-time buyers previously engaged through **October**; rebooked via portal into Paul’s diary). Paul:</p><p>  - produced a **DIP/certificate**</p><p>  - **CC’d Karen**</p><p>  - sent notes to **Karen and Megan**</p><p>**Vs Last Week / Last Month**</p><p>- Not stated.</p><p>**What’s Working Well**</p><p>- Good internal handover on Karen’s returning client (DIP produced; colleagues updated).</p><p>- Diary activity is increasing and building pipeline.</p><p>**What’s Not Working Well**</p><p>- Diary/lead reporting shows **0 this week** in one view despite **4 appointments held** → CRM/reporting alignment issue.</p><p>- Risk of appointment notes not being logged immediately, leaving “depth” appearing stale.</p><p>**Risks / Blockers**</p><p>- Incorrect visibility can create false “no leads” narratives and hides true workload/performance.</p><p>- If notes/tasks are not logged immediately, next steps are unclear and follow-ups drift.</p><p>**Actions Required**</p><p>- For each appointment: log **notes immediately**, set a **clear next step + dated task**, and confirm **correct ownership** (especially where the client belongs to **Karen/Megan**).</p><p>**Action Owner:** Adviser (Paul)</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **Stagnant (process), improving (activity)** — appointment volume is up; CRM/report accuracy remains inconsistent.</p><p>---</p><p>### 3. “Where Are My Leads?” (reporting inconsistency)</p><p>**Current Position**</p><p>- A reporting view shows **0 leads/appointments this week** while **4 appointments** were held by **2026-03-11**.</p><p>**Vs Last Week / Last Month**</p><p>- Not stated.</p><p>**What’s Working Well**</p><p>- Actual appointment activity exists and includes completed DIPs/notes sharing (Karen’s client).</p><p>**What’s Not Working Well**</p><p>- CRM/reporting misalignment undermines management information and adviser visibility.</p><p>**Risks / Blockers**</p><p>- Misreporting can drive the wrong performance narrative and mask workload; also increases the chance of missed follow-ups if tasks/notes are not correctly attached.</p><p>**Actions Required**</p><p>- Reconcile appointment activity vs CRM reporting views by ensuring each appointment has accurate **stage, owner, notes, and dated next-step tasks**.</p><p>**Action Owner:** Adviser (Paul)</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **Stagnant** — activity is present, but reporting integrity remains unreliable.</p><p>---</p><p>### 4. Applications in Progress (active cases mentioned)</p><p>&gt; Stages reflect discussion; several require CRM updates/notes to confirm exact status.</p><p>**Brianna Kennedy — NatWest — Application submitted**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Application submitted.</p><p>- **Delays / missing items:** CRM status/notes/report completion required to reflect submission accurately.</p><p>- **Risks / blockers:** Reporting inaccuracy until updated.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Update CRM stage + complete notes/report.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul) (Admin/Sean support as required).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **Improving** — blocker resolved and submission done; admin completion outstanding.</p><p>**Raymond — Remortgage (~£100k) — Docs received**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Docs received; moved back into today for progression.</p><p>- **Risks / blockers:** Drift risk if not actioned promptly now docs are in.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Progress to DIP/application as appropriate; update workflow and next steps.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **Improving** — documents now in, but requires immediate throughput.</p><p>**Graham Braid (Stephen Marlin’s old client) — Remortgage — Docs received**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Docs received; plan stated to “get that on this week”.</p><p>- **Risks / blockers:** Slippage due to competing workload.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Progress to submission; update CRM notes/tasks.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **At risk of decline** — intention stated but vulnerable to capacity constraints.</p><p>**Steph Mitchell — Product Transfer (Newcastle) — Stalled contact**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Stalled; should not be sitting overdue.</p><p>- **SLA breaches:** Identified as overdue/stalled with follow-up required.</p><p>- **Risks / blockers:** Lost retention and disengagement.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Adviser follow-up and workflow correction.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **Declining** — stalled contact and overdue position.</p><p>**Lloyd Dunlop — Residential (depth stage) — Docs received, needs booking**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Depth with docs received; booking not completed.</p><p>- **Risks / blockers:** Stalled at depth without movement.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Book appointment and progress to next stage; diarise next steps.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **Stagnant** — docs received but no progression.</p><p>**Mendon (American client) — Purchase (~£600k property) — Offer accepted**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Offer accepted; appointment scheduled immediately after the review.</p><p>- **Risks / blockers:** Needs documented next steps and affordability/DIP pathway.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Document appointment outcomes; progress to DIP/application pathway; log affordability and next steps.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **Improving** — appointment queued; execution pending.</p><p>**Edinburgh referral via Gavin — Residential purchase (~£1m property) — Docs received**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Docs received; booking required.</p><p>- **Risks / blockers:** High-value case; delays increase revenue/reputational exposure.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Book client; confirm requirements; progress to DIP.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **At risk of decline** — value is high; delay increases exposure.</p><p>**Jacob Juarez — Remortgage — Docs received, needs booking**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Docs received; booking not done.</p><p>- **Risks / blockers:** Delay due to adviser workload.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Schedule; progress to application; diarise.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **Stagnant** — awaiting booking.</p><p>**Unnamed remortgage — Submitted — proc fee ~£5,400–£5,500**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Submitted; **notes and report still required**.</p><p>- **SLA/compliance risk:** Compliance/audit trail exposure until completed.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Complete notes + report; update CRM stage.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **Declining (compliance risk)** — submission done but documentation outstanding.</p><p>**Buy-to-let remortgage — To be submitted**</p><p>- **Current stage:** Outstanding; not yet submitted.</p><p>- **Risks / blockers:** Slippage into next week if not prioritised.</p><p>- **Actions required:** Schedule and complete submission; update workflow.</p><p>- **Action owner:** Adviser (Paul).</p><p>- **Trend insight:** **At risk of decline** — dependent on capacity and prioritisation.</p><p>---</p><p>### 5. Protection in Progress (activity + conversion control)</p><p>**Current Position**</p><p>- Paul stated mortgage workload “will have an impact on protection penetration”.</p><p>- Protection items referenced:</p><p>  - Protection review booked with a client whose mortgage completed **last week Friday** (client name not stated).</p><p>  - **Angie Ray:** mortgage application + flagged for **Buildings &amp; Contents** discussion; **protection review scheduled on 2026-03-13**.</p><p>  - **Paul Clarkson:** protection review intent but client repeatedly goes silent; docs (ID etc.) requested; slow progress.</p><p>  - **Jamie Braithwaite:** protection quote completed (Sean); needs **health questions** + pushed to **provider/application site** (completion referenced as **mid April**).</p><p>  - **Wendy Watson:** needs progressing to **application**; needs insurance/health questions; priority stated **on risk ASAP**.</p><p>- Volume/mix discussed:</p><p>  - **Mortgage applications:** **13** currently + **7** still to add = **20 applications**</p><p>  - **Insurance cases:** **4**</p><p>  - Gap: converting roughly **16 mortgage cases** into additional protection outcomes.</p><p>**Vs Last Week / Last Month**</p><p>- Not stated.</p><p>**What’s Working Well**</p><p>- Protection review activity exists (e.g., **Angie Ray 2026-03-13**).</p><p>- Clear strategic focus that protection is the quarter differentiator; system exists to book **first protection appointments**.</p><p>**What’s Not Working Well**</p><p>- Protection is not consistently integrated into weekly output reporting (flagged by Bob).</p><p>- B&amp;C referral process not actioned:</p><p>  - **Angie Ray** not yet referred to **Megan** (Megan not aware).</p><p>  - System may be creating “fake” B&amp;C items under Paul, skewing stats.</p><p>- Control weak after first protection appointment:</p><p>  - Sean reports being “in the dark” after the first unless included.</p><p>  - Informal comms not consistently recorded/shared.</p><p>**Risks / Blockers**</p><p>- Protection income underperformance risk (only **4 insurance cases** vs mortgage pipeline moving towards **20**).</p><p>- Drift to completion without protection being finalised; “catch-up” behaviour later reduces conversion probability.</p><p>- On-risk delay:</p><p>  - **Wendy Watson** at risk if application/health questions slip.</p><p>  - **Jamie Braithwaite** at risk if not placed ahead of **mid April** completion.</p><p>**Actions Required**</p><p>- **Angie Ray:** confirm B&amp;C referral to **Megan** and correct CRM ownership.</p><p>- **Jamie Braithwaite + Wendy Watson:** Paul to push both to provider/application stage; Sean to complete health questions once live.</p><p>- **Paul + Sean:** agree and document a post-first-protection workflow (ownership, second appointment trigger, chasing rules, and how it is logged).</p><p>- Paul to **CC Sean** into relevant protection-booking/follow-up emails after mortgage application submission.</p><p>**Action Owner**</p><p>- Adviser (Paul) + Case Manager (Sean) + Other (Megan for B&amp;C)</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **At risk of declining** — mortgage momentum is rising, but protection follow-through and visibility after the first appointment is a repeated weak spot.</p><p>---</p><p>### 6. Written Business — This Month</p><p>**Current Position**</p><p>- March written so far: **£6k**.</p><p>- Expectation stated: potentially “on par” with last week (another **~£6k week** possible).</p><p>- February finish referenced: **£16.4k** written.</p><p>**Vs Last Week / Last Month**</p><p>- February written: **£16.4k** (full month); March currently **£6k** (partial).</p><p>**What’s Working Well**</p><p>- Strong momentum carrying into March; pipeline suggests continued writing.</p><p>**What’s Not Working Well**</p><p>- Throughput dependency: writing at risk if notes/reports and workflow follow-ups fall behind due to volume.</p><p>**Risks / Blockers**</p><p>- If protection conversion drops, total written may fall even if mortgage count remains strong.</p><p>- Compliance/admin drag (notes/reports) may slow submissions and completions.</p><p>**Actions Required**</p><p>- Protect writing capacity by delegating chase calls/workflow updates to **Sean** when diary becomes report-heavy.</p><p>**Action Owner:** Adviser (Paul) + Case Manager (Sean)</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **Improving** — strong start; key threat is operational drag from admin and missed follow-ups.</p><p>---</p><p>### 7. Banked Business / Banked Income (data available)</p><p>**Current Position**</p><p>- **February banked income:** just shy of **£13k**.</p><p>**Vs Last Week / Last Month**</p><p>- Not stated.</p><p>**What’s Working Well**</p><p>- Mortgage momentum is translating into banked income (February performance described as strong).</p><p>**What’s Not Working Well**</p><p>- Not stated.</p><p>**Risks / Blockers**</p><p>- Noted clawback exposure (no figures given) could reduce outlook.</p><p>**Actions Required**</p><p>- Not stated beyond existing workflow/protection actions to protect conversion and banking.</p><p>**Action Owner:** Adviser (Paul) + Case Manager (Sean)</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **Improving** — evidence of conversion into banked results; exposure exists if follow-through slips.</p><p>---</p><p>### 8. Renewal / Remortgage Income (pipeline + chasing discipline)</p><p>**Current Position**</p><p>- Renewals/remortgage opportunities in progress: **~4**.</p><p>- Named examples include **Steph Mitchell** and **Graham Braid**.</p><p>- **Graham Braid** noted as not originally Paul’s renewal client (linked to **Stephen Marlin**).</p><p>**Vs Last Week / Last Month**</p><p>- Not stated.</p><p>**What’s Working Well**</p><p>- Renewals pipeline described as “pretty positive”.</p><p>- Paul stated **one was put on today**.</p><p>**What’s Not Working Well**</p><p>- Visibility on broader renewal cohorts needs checking (referenced cohorts: **September / October / December** on remortgage spreadsheet).</p><p>- Potential ownership/reporting confusion where clients move between advisers.</p><p>**Risks / Blockers**</p><p>- Lost renewal income if spreadsheet cohorts are not actively chased and outcomes confirmed/recorded.</p><p>- False pipeline risk if clients have completed elsewhere and tracking is not updated.</p><p>**Actions Required**</p><p>- **Sean** to review remortgage spreadsheet and confirm status/outcomes for:</p><p>  - **September cohort (18 clients)**</p><p>  - **October and December cohorts** (counts not stated)</p><p>**Action Owner:** Case Manager (Sean)</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **Stagnant** — opportunities exist but spreadsheet-to-action clarity not evidenced within CRM outputs discussed.</p><p>---</p><p>### 9. Projected Income (pipeline outlook)</p><p>**Current Position**</p><p>- Projected income in pipeline:</p><p>  - **March:** just over **£6k** still to come</p><p>  - **April:** **£22k**</p><p>- Current “in view” total discussed: **~£40k** (approx **£12k banked + £22k April + ~£6k March**).</p><p>**Vs Last Week / Last Month**</p><p>- Not stated.</p><p>**What’s Working Well**</p><p>- Pipeline visibility is strong enough to quantify **~£40k** outlook.</p><p>**What’s Not Working Well**</p><p>- Income vulnerable to slippage if approvals do not land within the quarter (late March / early April writes only bank this quarter **if approved**).</p><p>**Risks / Blockers**</p><p>- Clawback exposure (no figures given).</p><p>- Protection not locked in early → quarter becomes mortgage-only and reduces total performance.</p><p>**Actions Required**</p><p>- Convert mortgage workload into protection revenue via tighter booking/follow-up control (see Protection + Workflow actions).</p><p>**Action Owner:** Adviser (Paul) + Case Manager (Sean)</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **Improving (with slippage risk)** — outlook is strong, but dependent on approvals and protection conversion discipline.</p><p>---</p><p>### 10. Overall Performance Diagnosis (performance data available)</p><p>**Adviser strengths this week**</p><p>- Strong mortgage pipeline: up to **6–7** applications anticipated; broader pipeline discussed as **13** currently + **7** to add (targeting **20**).</p><p>- Overdue workflow items reduced from **~20** to **12**.</p><p>- Good internal handover behaviour on Karen’s returning first-time buyer client (DIP produced; notes shared with **Karen and Megan**).</p><p>- Strong income visibility: **~£40k** projected in view; February banked just shy of **£13k**.</p><p>**Main risks**</p><p>- **CRM discipline risk:** **12** overdues; oldest due **2026-03-05** (6 days overdue), plus staging/ownership inaccuracies.</p><p>- **Protection conversion gap:** **4** insurance cases vs a mortgage pipeline moving towards **20** applications; weak control after the first protection appointment.</p><p>- **Throughput/compliance bottleneck:** **6–7 notes/reports** outstanding; submitted case with proc fee **~£5,400–£5,500** still needs notes/report.</p><p>**Behaviour patterns**</p><p>- When busy, follow-ups drift and protection is deprioritised; cases are left “in place” rather than delegated/escalated.</p><p>- Informal comms not consistently recorded/shared → visibility gap for Sean after first protection meeting.</p><p>- Reporting inconsistencies (appointments held vs “0 this week” view) reduce control and accountability.</p><p>**Accountability concerns**</p><p>- Continued tolerance of follow-ups exceeding **6 days overdue**.</p><p>- Delayed escalation to Sean for chase calls during capacity constraints.</p><p>- B&amp;C referral ownership not corrected (e.g., **Angie Ray** not yet referred to **Megan**).</p><p>**Where management/support is required**</p><p>- Sean to take rapid chase calls + workflow updates for “radio silence/property hunting” cases to prevent drift.</p><p>- Agree and apply a joint Paul/Sean protection process after the first appointment.</p><p>- Clarify B&amp;C referral process so ownership/reporting is accurate (Megan visibility).</p><p>**Trend Insight:** **Improving overall, but fragile** — pipeline and income outlook are strong, but CRM discipline and protection follow-through could quickly reverse gains.</p><p>---</p><p>## 12. Master Action List</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Complete notes + report for the remortgage application already submitted (proc fee **~£5,400–£5,500**) and update CRM stage | Compliance / Submitted case | Adviser (Paul) | 2026-03-11 | Compliance exposure; poor audit trail; reporting inaccuracies |</p><p>| Push protection cases to provider/application stage so health questions can be completed | **Jamie Braithwaite**; **Wendy Watson** | Adviser (Paul) | 2026-03-11 | Delayed placement; Wendy not on risk ASAP; Jamie risks missing pre-completion window (mid April) |</p><p>| Complete protection health questions once cases are live on provider site | **Jamie Braithwaite**; **Wendy Watson** | Case Manager (Sean) | 2026-03-11 | Application stalls; cover delayed; protection income delayed |</p><p>| Clear or re-task all **12 overdue** workflow items; no item to remain **&amp;gt;2 days overdue** without documented decision | CRM discipline | Adviser (Pau