t then what&#x27;s happening here is, and what it&#x27;ll do is, because it&#x27;s you&#x27;ve created it under yourself, It&#x27;s assuming that you&#x27;re the one that&#x27;s going to be doing the B and C review. Now if you&#x27;re then going to pass that to Megan, effectively that needs to be updated so it shows as Megan.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So then that then doesn&#x27;t pull through in your stats, yeah.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Well, don&#x27;t know. That seems to be a disparity between Midas and the reports. Sorry, I only got. Um, because when you select B and C review, it says are you doing the review? And I select no. And then it says who&#x27;s doing it? And I&#x27;ll select Megan.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah. I know and it doesn&#x27;t filter through into the lead details. It doesn&#x27;t at all. Yeah, I know that yeah, Um, So it&#x27;s just something that&#x27;s I&#x27;m saying it&#x27;s something you need to be conscious of is if you are going to pass that to Megan, is just make sure you&#x27;re updating the lead details. So as actually Megan, that&#x27;s going to be the one that&#x27;s picking up. Yeah. Um, Last week. And but even still actually, if we take, you know that one away actually out of it, ten leads, which I think is quite nice. Um, It&#x27;s been a while since I think you&#x27;ve probably had a good ten in your diary.</p><p>Bob Duncan: And actually, the vast majority look like you are kind of in touch with, which is quite good. This week, where are we? You&#x27;ve not got any this week. Is that right?</p><p>Paul Banyard: Three on Monday. I did two yesterday, but one of them technically will be for Karen. Okay, they&#x27;d spoken to Karen. First time buyers, she was in contact with them right up until October last year. Um and then nothing since then. And then I think they just booked in an appointment themselves through the portal, and it just went into my diary. Um but I&#x27;ve let Karen know, spoke to them yesterday, they were viewing a property last night. I got them a debt certificate CC&#x27;d in Karen, sent Karen and Megan notes about.</p><p>Paul Banyard: This is what we discussed in terms of income, blah blah blah. So that&#x27;s with Karen. So yeah, but yeah, I mean my diary is saying one two three. Uh well three yesterday as no we&#x27;re on Wednesday aren&#x27;t we? Sorry three on Monday. One yesterday,</p><p>Bob Duncan: So four, very bizarreum let&#x27;s go and have a look at your depths.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Oh. So nice to see that we&#x27;ve only got three appointment booked and created. We know about one of those created, so probably just two there that appointment booked. So, and again it might be a B and C type thing there. Yeah,</p><p>Paul Banyard: That one for Brianna Kennedy. I&#x27;ve already updated that after I did the application when you upload the product, so on your report tomorrow, that will show as applications submitted.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Good. Um, so back to our depth sense. So we&#x27;ve got twelve at depth stage. I guess probably where we are with with these ones. Um, it doesn&#x27;t look like we&#x27;ve updated any notes on them since we last spoke. Um, So I would still take this as as this still to be actioned this one?</p><p>Paul Banyard: Well, I got them all up to date. Um, just some of them are either not actively looking immediately, Um or you know, they were up to date a week or two ago. And then you know, it&#x27;ll be a couple of weeks before I check in with them again because they&#x27;re not, you know, viewing properties every week essentially. Okay. You know based on when they want contact.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Okay so they&#x27;re kind of slow burners these ones yeah?</p><p>Paul Banyard: Yeah I mean that one there Lloyd Dunlop, I&#x27;ve got that&#x27;s one of the cases I mentioned earlier. I&#x27;ve got documents in, just need to get them booked in, so that&#x27;ll be progressing.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Okay, good. And let&#x27;s have a look at your written. See where you&#x27;re now sitting. So nice strong finish for February, Um sixteen four, means your March and April is a bit easier on the written target, and you&#x27;re currently off to a strong start. With six K and potentially what another four K five K this week maybe more, um.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Might be on par. The last week made another six K week maybe. So, Do you know I think that&#x27;s.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Do you know I think you have to nail in the head at the start of this. You&#x27;ve got lots of mortgages. The key thing here is to make sure the process between you and Sean is nailed tight for the insurance, Because we need to make sure that are, you know are capitalizing, and we&#x27;ll look at some of the numbers later on. But. I think it&#x27;s really quite interesting if you look at, you know, if you look at last month, you know how much of that was protection income. 10 grand in protection income, five grand in mortgage income and 1200 quid in admin fees. So, you can actually see straight away, even if you break it down right throughout the year. The protection income, if you look at it, Paul is the biggest chunk of the money that you&#x27;re going to make.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Always tends to be on par or higher than the mortgage business. Well,</p><p>Bob Duncan: This is it. And, if you take the time to do it and you really nail the process, then you can really do well there. So just, you know, you had a great month in February. Let&#x27;s just keep an eye on the numbers and we&#x27;ll check them shortly. But I&#x27;d hate for us to lose that momentum that you&#x27;ve been building over the last probably three months. So let&#x27;s just really keep a close eye on that one, yeah? And if we look at, in fact, can we go year on year? Yeah.</p><p>Speaker 4: So you can see there, Paul straight away. Um, you know, 114 written last year or 114 written so far this year. Um and you&#x27;ve still got a couple of months to go. So you&#x27;re going to probably if you look at the numbers, you&#x27;re probably going to smash what you wrote last year.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Probably quite comfortably.</p><p>Paul Banyard: What&#x27;s last year? Does that report tell you?</p><p>Bob Duncan: It doesn&#x27;t say. I&#x27;m kind of assuming it was a hundred because it says total revenues. Hundred, yeah, so one hundred k last year. You&#x27;re on one hundred and fourteen this year, and you&#x27;ve still got two months to go. And you&#x27;re hoping to put in another thirty k, maybe plus on top of that. So, um. Yeah, you&#x27;ve definitely got the momentum. It feels like the momentum&#x27;s there. I think would you agree?</p><p>Paul Banyard: Uhhuh. Certainly does uh like the past few weeks yeahum, You always get to a point like if I bang through them, seven odd, mortgages. You&#x27;re always then sitting going right? Oh, well, that&#x27;s sold my pipeline on what what am I going to get next, but there&#x27;s always stuff that falls in. Yeah, I mean you go from having minimal pipeline to having like 12 or 13 mortgages to put on in the space of two weeks? So yeah, so definitely the momentum&#x27;s there.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Well, this is it I think it&#x27;s just maintaining it. And this is where, you know, this relationship between you and Sean becomes really quite key. You know, last week, I think, you know, he said to Sean. No, could you key some cases? You know, we shouldn&#x27;t get to Wednesday. And a nice way for you to ask that question. You know, you probably need to just have a bit of a sensible head to go. And I&#x27;ve got seven cases to key this week. What&#x27;s what&#x27;s the sensible approach here? Sean, could you actually support with that? Yeah, Because then that gives you, well, yeah time back to get, you know, get your head in gear, because you know, you need to. It&#x27;s a real juggling act of.</p><p>Bob Duncan: You don&#x27;t want to block your diary out because you&#x27;re wanting your appointments because you want the pipeline to keep building. But if you completely block out, then you aren&#x27;t going to get the pipeline and you&#x27;re going to slow down. So, when you do get busy like you are this week, if you&#x27;ve got cases and PTS is a thirty second job. Okay, so it&#x27;s a five ten minute job, right, but Sean could do it in five ten minutes. Yeah. And it&#x27;s really good experience for Sean when he then moves over that he&#x27;s getting this kind of wider experience. So it gives you ten minutes back. But it&#x27;s ten minutes you didn&#x27;t have to then go and do something else, yeah. Okay, so don&#x27;t be afraid to ask the question to Sean and Sean, you will be honest and say &quot;bull&quot; or actually more than happy to help. Yeah, I think, maybe the one bit of feedback will be if you&#x27;re going to tell them, they key a depth in an app with a lender, make sure it&#x27;s the right one. So we&#x27;re not doing double the work even though it played out in your favor. Yeah.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Even though it played out in your favor, if it hadn&#x27;t, that&#x27;d have been a waste of an hour&#x27;s time. Yeah. And.</p><p>Paul Banyard: He was doing.</p><p>Bob Duncan: You know your bullshit. Bullshit. Um banked again, if we then just kind of flip that back and we talk about your written poll. So all that effort that you are starting to put in nowum is starting to kind of paid dividends on your banked income, you know see what a cracking bank for February just shy thirteen. I&#x27;d be hoping to see sort of similar in March, similar in April as long as that trajectory keeps going. And a good indication of that will probably be our projected income. So what have you got kind of coming down the line? There&#x27;s Paul. So yeah, we can see it. March has only shown up just over six K still to come, but April with 22.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So you&#x27;ve got twelve in the bank already, plus another twenty two. So that&#x27;s thirty four, plus another six puts you on roughly forty k as it stands, give or take. Also keep in mind, any mortgages you are writing for the rest of March and partially part of April. If they get approved, we will probably bank in this quarter as well. So you&#x27;ve got as it stands forty eight forty k. Banked income sitting there, give or take. Assuming that you don&#x27;t have any massive clawbacks, You&#x27;ve actually got the potential to really probably ramp that up a wee bit more as well. And you know, so just you know good number of mortgages which is great. Let&#x27;s have a look to see how that moves over into probably the stats because that&#x27;s probably the next part of the jigsaw. So.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So take where you are. So you&#x27;ve got 13 applications, you&#x27;ve got another seven to stick on. So you&#x27;re going to be 20 applications, Paul, yeah? And four insurances. So, you can see straight away now that insurance gap is going to be your kind of big challenge. Probably for the next few weeks. It&#x27;s, how do you take those effectively 16 cases? And how do we then get some insurance business out of them? I would maybe say is a good starting point out of these thirteen that we&#x27;ve got on. Where are we at with first appointments or second appointments? That&#x27;s probably a sit down and a chat for the two of you. Where are we at with those? Um, and then once that next seven go on, Obviously, just making sure that you&#x27;re on top of those cases to get them booked in for their first appointments. The minute the mortgage offers are out or sooner rather than later, depending on what you&#x27;re doing. Yeah.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Mhm. Yep. Yeah, &#x27;cause I mean, I know it says 13 at the moment, but if I bet if you were to add, you know what what would 13? Six point five. You know, if you add one case it&#x27;ll really ramp up your numbers. You know, you add one case should almost 40%, Paul. Mhm. Um If you add two cases your 46%. So, you know one or two cases makes it look good if that makes sense? But if you had seven mortgages, your numbers are going to look quite the opposite. So just be very conscious. I would say is, I would say, probably the big focus for the two of you next week is insurance first and insurance follow ups. Yeah, has to be looking at those numbers there. Yeah.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Yeah, Well, yeah or hopefully in a way, I mean not to say that I don&#x27;t want more mortgage pipeline, butum not the numbers that I&#x27;ve got like. Last week and this week, something a bit calmer, you know. One or two, um. Yeah, And that then allows me to focus on, you know, the mortgages are done for these, you know, six odd cases last week, seven this week. Now let&#x27;s focus on uh getting the protection on for.</p><p>Paul Banyard: As many of them as we can. That&#x27;s it. I think if you talk to the manager, it&#x27;s like a shift from mortgage to protection focus. Yeah, Not not to say that there isn&#x27;t any focus on protection;. It&#x27;s obviously discussed during the mortgage discussion, but yeah, just pinning them down.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, Because I mean, you know, you know, as we listed where six insurance cases you&#x27;re looking for. And if you maintain your premium one hundred and twenty six poundsum and you get those on that&#x27;s. You know, it&#x27;s a staggering sixteen grand in income, Paul. Yeah. You know, So that&#x27;s why I am saying you like, don&#x27;t really make sure the two of you are talking on these cases. Where are we? Are we booking in straight away for the protection review? Or are we waiting for the mortgage offer? Which, is it we&#x27;re doing and then making sure that these people are booked in at the appropriate time? And really try to maintain it. Yeah, because if you let it slip now while you are busy.</p><p>Bob Duncan: You&#x27;re then always going to be trying to play catch up further down the line, yeah. And if we look at where you finished in Q three, So sixty six percent overall average protection case was quite low, actually for Q three fifty one fifty four thousand pounds average mortgages is what it is. So just under fifteen hundred quid. But you&#x27;ve obviously got off to a better start. But if we look at your year to date stats.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Are we forty five point three But your average year to date is ninety, so it just looks like Q three was probably. Yeah, see Q two was fine. Q one was slightly behind on a way I&#x27;m not yeah. That was okay in Q one. So it just seems your Q three premiums have been slightly lower than they&#x27;ve normally been. Um, you seem to be back on track in Q four, which is quite nice, but. As I say, let&#x27;s not let momentum basically drop here. That would be my biggest worry is you&#x27;ve got sixteen grand there, and you&#x27;re going to put on five grand this week. I mean, if you got that all in this month, that&#x27;s a twenty six grand month. Yeah, twenty six twenty eight grands. I know you probably won&#x27;t get it all on, But have the focus to go get as much as we can on. Yeah, yes, um.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Renewals. Still pretty positive though.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, no, I think it&#x27;s really positive for us.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Top for mortgages, equal top for protection so far. But yeah,</p><p>Bob Duncan: No, I think you&#x27;re having a fantastic quarter. I think, well, actually I think you&#x27;ve got the potential to maybe even you know, catch up and take over Claire. If you keep your momentum up on the mortgages. You know. So mortgages is kind of where you were lagging behind against, you know, Claireum and you&#x27;ve got seven to put on this week. So you know, you&#x27;re pretty much going to be. The same as clear. I don&#x27;t know what the value is, but actually from a mortgage perspective, you&#x27;re going to be maybe there or there about on mortgage income. Differentiator will be in here is the admin fee. Um and then if we look at oh excuse me, you&#x27;re in the way here. I need to move here. Um and then your neck and neck on insurance. So you know if you get all these mortgages on.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, I have the potential to kind of pep clear. But, the big one will be is fees. You know if you&#x27;ve charged fees or not charged fees, because that&#x27;s one thing that will kind of separate you slightly. And it&#x27;s separating you. Yeah. Mhm, yeah, but you&#x27;ll know that yourself. Um renewals. So I think you mentioned obviously got quite a few renewal opportunities coming through. So how many renewal opportunities are you working at the moment?</p><p>Paul Banyard: I put one on today. Two, three, three right now. Yeah, well, Stephanie Mitchell&#x27;s four. Five? I&#x27;d say four. Four, but yeah, One of them was an opportunity just came out of the blue because Graham Braid was not my renewal client ;. It was. Stephen Marlin, so it just happened to come to me. About as lucky, I&#x27;ll take it.</p><p>Bob Duncan: That&#x27;s my client as well. God damn it, you guys are picking up all my old clients. What can I say? Indeed, So I guess probably I&#x27;ve got a question for both of you to look at, then is probably Sean, You&#x27;ll probably need to look at the remortgage spreadsheet. But if I&#x27;m kind of looking at this kind of September bunch here. Where are with these ones in September? How. Many have we actually got written business Same with our October Same with our December So. We can just look at the renewal spreadsheet and go Where. Are with these eighteen clients Cause I&#x27;d be hoping to see this business trickle in this year Um So Sean. If you can have a little look at that and just see where we are roughly with those making sure that we&#x27;re still chasing them up Um.</p><p>Bob Duncan: And if you know, if they&#x27;ve gone off and done something, that&#x27;s fine. But let&#x27;s just make sure that. If they&#x27;re still on a chaser, let&#x27;s try to chase them. Yeah.</p><p>Paul Banyard: So with that says six in September, Is that saying that uh six mortgages we&#x27;ve previously written are on a product that will end in September? No, no, no.</p><p>Bob Duncan: That is saying that they will end in six months&#x27;time from September from September. So they will end in March and then your October will be your April, Your December will be your your May, yeah. Why.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Would you want to look at those now? Because you.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Well, we should we should have been like we should have had a first point of contact in September, first point of contact in October,</p><p>Speaker 4: First point oh twenty twenty five. Sorry, yeah,</p><p>Bob Duncan: So it&#x27;s you know. So just so the theory it should be dropping in this year or setting a good chunk of these and I. Guess the question is, is you know where are we on the the list? You know they definitely getting chased yeah. Yeah they&#x27;re getting, Yeah, make sense. And then if you look at next year, then you&#x27;re going to have a quite a strong year. I know we kind of touch on this all the time, but if you think about September, the chunk of that&#x27;s going to come through in December. So you&#x27;re actually going to have probably quite a busy December, More busier than you normally would find just because you&#x27;ve got quite a bit of potential business. You might be trying to get off your desk before Christmas as well.</p><p>Paul Banyard: It&#x27;s kind of quite good because uh, You know, seasonally purchase cases drop off a cliff in December, So at least you&#x27;ve got some renewal business, you can rattle through and still have a decent month for written business.</p><p>Bob Duncan: That&#x27;s it exactly. At call, And that&#x27;s how you&#x27;ve got to look at it, because if you then look at it and go right, well, September ten grand in business and where was written.</p><p>Bob Duncan: What&#x27;s you&#x27;re going to have,</p><p>Paul Banyard: You know, a handful of purchases that still go ahead in like November December. So even though it drops off a cliff, there&#x27;s always something that you&#x27;re going to put on.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Well if you look at December last year, you done six and if you even picked up fifty percent of that, that puts on pitch on eleven and a half call. Yeah yeah. And you would like to think that your momentum is just going to build year on year over the Christmas period, so seasonality starts to become. It will still show you will still have those peaks, but they will not be as drastic. Um. As they have been yeah. Yeah okay. Um what else I think that might be us just gonna quickly check yep. So I think the key takeaways for me there.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Apologies, my camera keeps going off. I have no idea why. Um, key takeaways is workflows. Paul, you need to make sure you&#x27;re on top of those. Um, if you can&#x27;t manage it, You need to make sure you&#x27;re asking Sean to do those check ins for you. It&#x27;s a quick five minutes. Um, There&#x27;s a few um statuses that need updated. So we can just have a quick check of those. And other than that, it&#x27;s again probably just saying to Sean, if you are too busy. Actually, Sean, can you pick up some applications for me and key them? And if he&#x27;s got the scope, he will do them. But don&#x27;t leave it as a last minute dot com. Yeah. Because if you can get all the prep work done and dusted, Then I&#x27;ll make your life that wee bit easier towards the end of the week. Because Sean like you will have peaks and troughs. There will be days that he&#x27;s quite busy. There&#x27;ll be days that he&#x27;s quite quiet when he makes mistakes on properties for me, it creates more work for him.</p><p>Bob Duncan: But it&#x27;s all learning for Sean.</p><p>Sean McLennan: Yeah, that would be fine. Your.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Face when you worked out what you&#x27;d done wrong was a picture.</p><p>Sean McLennan: I just get furious at myself for silly things.</p><p>Bob Duncan: It&#x27;s attention to detail, and it sounds easy to say that, but it&#x27;s also just trying to juggle so many cases. And knowing exactly each client what they&#x27;re doing, what they&#x27;re thinking, and when you have your own portfolio of clients, Paul, you&#x27;ll maybe agree with this. It is actually much easier to manage. Yeah. Whereas if you&#x27;ve had to deal with three, you&#x27;ve got.</p><p>Sean McLennan: Yeah because you will have done the recommendation, You would have talked through the products, whereas for me I&#x27;m coming into afterwards. And then going like, oh, that was a five year. Yeah, whatever yeah.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So it&#x27;s like worked out. Indeed, so some actions there. So quite a positive, probably I think really start to this quarter. Paul, I think you&#x27;ve as I say, I think you have got real potential this quarter to probably pip clear if momentum keeps going. But the key to that has to be your insurance. Yeah, really got to maintain that between the two of you. So whatever that process was which as soon as the recommendation&#x27;s done, get them booked in for the insurance first one.</p><p>Bob Duncan: We need to make sure that that&#x27;s happening. Yeah, you got to keep that on track, keep the momentum. Then once that is done, what&#x27;s happening for the second appointment How are we moving that forward Yeah.</p><p>Sean McLennan: What I would what I would actually bring up is I think we&#x27;ve got a really good system for the firsts. So offer out either Paul or me. We&#x27;ll bring it up on our catch up like what&#x27;s happening Who&#x27;s calling them Who&#x27;s getting it booked in Um, and we get it in for the first. I think after that first appointment for protection has happened, thenum maybe a little bit more in the dark about what&#x27;s going on. Obviously there&#x27;s notes there and stuff like that, butum just so that we don&#x27;t let them slip away, it goes to completion and then you&#x27;re chasing them. So,</p><p>Bob Duncan: The two of you should maybe have a powwow and decide how you&#x27;re going to manage that journey then. Um.</p><p>Sean McLennan: Would you agree Paul or is that just me? Because you could be in contact with them and I am just not aware, But I don&#x27;t know if you want me to chase people and stuff at them.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Um, well, I think there is maybe an easy fix there, which is if I am communicating with a client, say via email, which I would be even if I speak to him on the phone. I&#x27;ll usually back that up with an email, so I can add it to notes. Um once i&#x27;ve submitted a mortgage application, they&#x27;ve been introduced to you, so they know that you&#x27;re. The case manager, if you like, And so I can just make sure I CC you into these sorts of communications. If I am trying to get them booked in or you know whatever it is. Yeah, so you&#x27;ve got sight of that. Yeah. But yeah, ultimately they should be in the case notes. But yeah, I&#x27;ll admit, sometimes there is just a little bit of back and forth with clients that&#x27;s more informal. And it&#x27;s like, well, I am not going to add that to case notes because it&#x27;s not.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Not that relevant, but I know everything is supposed to go on case notes, But there&#x27;s this occasionally little tidbits of information that you don&#x27;t want MAP to be reading. That&#x27;s fine.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, no, We&#x27;ll just keep an eye on that then because I think that&#x27;s going to be quite fundamental. Um, I don&#x27;t want you to slip back after we&#x27;ve got you moving in the right direction. You do have two cases that do need to beum keyed, Um, on to the lender sites. You&#x27;ve got Jamie Braithwaite, which you&#x27;ve done the quote for Sean. You need to do the health questions for Jamie, and then Wendy Watson needs to be progressed as an application and Sean, you need to do the insurance questions with Wendy as well. Um so those are two extra cases Be fifty quid It&#x27;s not massive um, but we do need those two applications on. So somebody just needs to key them.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Um, don&#x27;t know who that is either.</p><p>Paul Banyard: One Wendy,</p><p>Bob Duncan: Who Wendy Watson? You produced a quote the other day for it, Paul. I do need to email Wendy back and which I will doum just with the kind of plan of action. But, my week&#x27;s been a bit of a nightmare as it is every week. Yeah,</p><p>Paul Banyard: If you yeah, if you email and say, right, this is what we&#x27;ve agreed. This is the protection we&#x27;re going to put in place. And I&#x27;m CC&#x27;d in then I&#x27;ll know yeah. To proceed. Well, I just did the application live for both of them, so Sean can go and do the health questions.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Right. So, if you can take five minutes to push it to application or push it through to the provider site, and then Sean can do theum health questions for both of them because Jamie completes at the end of the week, end of the month.</p><p>Sean McLennan: Uh mid April.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Mid April. And Wendy&#x27;s looking to get her on risk ASAP.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Great, cool. Okay, so those are our actions. I&#x27;ve got a mortgage sign up right now, but I&#x27;ll try and do it after that. Cool. Once I&#x27;ve worked my overdue up.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Anything else from either of you? Any positives, negatives, frustrations? I know you&#x27;ve got a call, but kind of just really quickly conscious to hear anything that&#x27;s from either side.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Uh no, completely unrelated. Um I would imagine that you&#x27;re in talks with the board about uh pay scales for the new financial year coming up. Yeah. Can I assume that I&#x27;m not going to get a pay rise for year for the third or fourth year on on the trot?</p><p>Bob Duncan: That&#x27;ll be, I actually don&#x27;t know the answer to that Paul, it&#x27;s the honest answer. Um I don&#x27;t know. I don&#x27;t know what&#x27;s happening this year for for pay rises, there&#x27;s nothing been mentioned. Company level at all at this stage, or at an FS level. Fair enough. Okay, sure that will come in due course. Yeah, once I know I will let you know. So watch this space. Yeah, cool okay right excellent anything for you Sean? No all good cool right? Paul sounds like you got a crackingly busy week, so get cracking.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Get that business on. Looking forward to see that number sitting at twelve next week. Okay, cheers Bye, bye.</p></body></html>